<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * Outgoing mail settings -- EXAMPLE FILE.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * COPY THIS TO php/config/mail.php AND FILL IN YOUR OWN VALUES.
 *
 *     Copy-Item php\config\mail.php.example php\config\mail.php
 *
 * The real file is excluded by .gitignore because it holds a password. This one
 * is committed in its place, and it must never contain a real credential --
 * if you fill this file in instead of the copy, you will commit your password.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * To send real email you need a Google App Password. Gmail stopped accepting
 * ordinary account passwords from third-party programs in 2022, so your normal
 * Google password will simply be rejected here.
 *
 *   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification for the Google account:
 *      https://myaccount.google.com/security
 *      Google will not offer app passwords until this is on.
 *
 *   2. Create an app password:
 *      https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
 *      You get 16 characters, shown as four groups like "abcd efgh ijkl mnop".
 *      The spaces are only there to make it readable -- keep them or drop them,
 *      both work.
 *
 *   3. Fill in GMAIL_ADDRESS and GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD below, then change
 *      MAIL_TRANSPORT to 'smtp'.
 *
 *   4. Check it worked, from the project folder:
 *          php php/tools/mail-test.php your.name@gmail.com
 *
 * THE SENDER CAN ALSO BE CHANGED WITHOUT EDITING THIS FILE, as of 2026-10-11.
 * php/admin/settings.php writes the address, the app password and the sender
 * name into the `settings` table, and php/config/settings.php prefers those
 * over the constants here -- so these three are the documented default and the
 * admin screen is an override. A stored value wins; clearing it falls back to
 * what is below. MAIL_TRANSPORT is deliberately NOT editable there: it decides
 * whether verification codes reach anybody at all.
 */

/**
 * 'log'  -- write messages to MAIL_LOG_FILE instead of sending them. The whole
 *           sign-in flow works; you read the code out of that file. Start here.
 * 'smtp' -- really send, through smtp.gmail.com. Needs the two constants below.
 *
 * NOTE FOR RUNNING THE TEST SUITES: php/tools/orders-http-test.php reads the
 * log to check what a customer was told, so on 'smtp' it skips those seven
 * assertions and says so. Set this to 'log' to test the wording of the mail.
 */
const MAIL_TRANSPORT = 'log';

/** The Gmail account doing the sending, e.g. 'your.name@gmail.com'. */
const GMAIL_ADDRESS = '';

/** The 16-character app password from step 2 above -- NOT your Google password. */
const GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD = '';

/** Shown as the sender's name in the recipient's inbox. */
const MAIL_FROM_NAME = 'Sky and LJ\'s Craft';

/**
 * Temporarily bypass login 2FA for ONE admin account; empty this to restore it.
 *
 * Leave it empty. It exists for working on the admin screens without reading a
 * code out of the log on every sign-in, and an account listed here signs in on
 * a password alone -- no emailed code, no trusted-device check. On the account
 * that can edit every product, settle every payment and read every customer,
 * with no login rate limiting anywhere in this project, that is the whole shop
 * behind one password. See OPEN ISSUES in ROADMAP.md.
 */
const ADMIN_LOGIN_2FA_BYPASS_EMAIL = '';

/**
 * Where the 'log' transport writes.
 *
 * Deliberately in the system temp folder rather than anywhere under htdocs:
 * these messages contain live verification codes, and a file inside the web root
 * could be opened by anyone who guessed the URL.
 */
define('MAIL_LOG_FILE', rtrim(sys_get_temp_dir(), '\\/') . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR . 'capstone-mail.log');

const SMTP_HOST = 'smtp.gmail.com';
const SMTP_PORT = 587;

/** Seconds to wait on the connection and on each reply before giving up. */
const SMTP_TIMEOUT = 15;
